 Adds an optional merge hook to committer files so close-out commits onl… [skip ci]\n167c3df ard(triage:engineer): #142 Refuses any worker change to a `.gitattributes` file in the committer's… [skip ci]\nf4f08f6 Refresh docs/TODO.md (#190)\n4cf2b20 Add ard serve --loops; the soak runs only scanners, stale and reaper (#187) (#188)\n348cf1c Cost and tokens table on each issue (#172, part 2) (#189)\na7027c7 ard(triage:lead): #89 Adds a second, optional pass to the close-out secret scan: a new scanne… [skip ci]\ncdeccea ard(triage:lead): #142 The fix is to make a worker change to any .gitattributes file go to a h… [skip ci]\n4928ed5 ard(triage:lead): #138 Close-out should stop a job's plan.md commit from reverting sections th… [skip ci]\ndf240ba chore: Wake the loops without restarting ard serve (#161) (#186)\n81a7b13 Refresh docs/TODO.md (#185)\nb7926e2 chore: Job cleanup fails on Go's read-only module cache and leak… (#174) (#181)\n1d7570d feat: serve: refuse to start on a git too old for the committer (#165) (#180)\n26ba2aa Add Phase 4 (docs and site) and Phase 5 (distribution) (#179)\nbfabfc6 chore: eng-review should run the repository's own lint target (#160) (#176)\nbbfe3a5 Record per-step usage and commit a per-job costs file (#172, part 1) (#182)\n30b476c Add the reviewer loop (#35)\nCLAUDE.md\nDockerfile\nLICENSE\nMakefile\nREADME.md\ncmd\ndeploy\ndocs\ngo.mod\ngo.sum\ninternal\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n258:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reaper), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n259-\n260-A worker job is several executor jobs (`<id>`, `<id>t1`, `<id>f1`, ...) under one state job and one slot; the Reaper watches whichever step is stored on the job, so its heartbeat and deadline rules apply to tests too.\n261-\n262-**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected.\n263-\n264-|  | Local executor | Kubernetes executor |\n265-| --- | --- | --- |\n266-| Workspace | `git worktree` of the controller's clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`) under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/<job-id>`; sidecar exported into the job home | `Job` with an init container that shallow-clones the branch into an `emptyDir`; sidecar cloned by an init container (Phase 3) |\n267-| Runtime | the role's `Dockerfile` image under Docker if it has one, else a host subprocess; hard timeout | a pod from the role's `Dockerfile` image, or the default `ard-agent` image; hard timeout |\n268-| Heartbeat | PID alive plus log file mtime | pod phase plus container restarts |\n269-| Logs | streamed to `~/.ard/logs/<owner>/<repo>/<issue#>/` while running; committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` at close-out | pod logs, committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` at close-out |\n270-| Labels on resources | job id in the directory name | `ard/issue`, `ard/stage`, `ard/role`, `ard/job` |\n271-| Good for | Phase 1, one host, fast iteration | parallelism, isolation, untrusted inputs |\n272-\n273-**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n274-\n275-**The agent's contract is narrow.** It may edit files in its worktree and must write `.ard/<issue#>/<stage>-<role>.md` with a `status`. Under `.ard/<issue#>/` it may change only its own role file and what its role's allowlist grants (see the `.ard/` contract). Exit 0 without that file counts as a failure, not a success, and so does a malformed file. The file counts only if this job wrote it: before the agent starts, the executor removes the job's role file from the worktree (`executor.Job.Scrub`), so a copy left from an earlier stage or run is never trusted. A role file the agent never wrote again fails the job (`role file not written by this job`, or `role file missing` when none was ever there). One it wrote again with identical bytes still counts, but only because the executor recorded the scrub in the handle (`Handle.Scrubbed`); close-out refuses an unscrubbed, unchanged role file (`role file not scrubbed by executor`). A non-zero exit fails the job even when a role file exists, and a failed job commits only its redacted log, never a partial `plan.md`.\n276-\n277-**Close-out is resumable.** The controller (`internal/closeout`) decides the outcome from the role file (only one this job wrote), stages the redacted files, then commits, requests the `next` labels, posts one summary comment (for a review with a verdict, one PR review instead; either is tagged `<!-- ard:closeout job=<id> -->` so a replay never posts twice) and finally swaps `:running` for `:done`, `:changes`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:executor:failed`. It records its phase in local state after each step. `serve` resumes unfinished close-outs at startup, and the Reaper resumes, rather than fails, a job whose close-out has started. Three more outcomes exist for a job that ended without a usable result: `failed` (sets `ard:executor:failed`), `retry` (a named transient cause: no `ard:executor:failed`, the request label kept, the next claim is the automatic retry) and `usage-limit` (a stop at a subscription usage limit: `:running` is removed, nothing is added, the request label stays, and the issue is claimed again after the reset without using up the retry). All three commit only the redacted log. Their comment carries the reason, the last 50 log lines (cut from the redacted log, scanned again and capped at 8 KiB; if that scan flags or fails, the comment says the tail was withheld) and when the worktree and host files are kept until. `next` entries are applied only for roles that exist; a `review:<role>` the lead requests is applied when the worker opens the PR, not at triage close-out. Only changed files under `.ard/<issue#>/` that the job's allowlist permits are committed, so a newer `issue.md` on `main` is never overwritten.\n278-\n279-**Usage is recorded for every job, whatever its outcome.** The Finisher stores each agent step's usage (the models it reported, the four token counts, input, output, cache write and cache read, and the cost) in local state, keyed by job and executor step id, so a replay or a Reaper re-parse never counts a step twice. A worker job's agent and fix steps are summed: the job's totals come from state, not from the last step. A failed, retried or usage-limited job keeps its cost, and the totals go into the `reaper.finish` log. Close-out commits the job's tracking file `.ard/<issue#>/costs/<job-id>.json` (stage, role, attempt, outcome, cause, models, tokens by type, cost, start and end time, PR) in the job's normal lane in the same commit as its log: `main` for triage, the PR branch for worker and reviewer jobs. It is controller-written and holds no free text. The cost is the agent CLI's list-price estimate, not a bill; a job whose adapter reported none records `null`. **The issue body carries a cost table.** Reaching `closed` marks the issue's cost table dirty in the same state transaction, and the `costs` loop (one minute, one writer per issue) writes it: it renders the table from state (one row per closed job, oldest first, \"#k\" for the k-th run of a stage and role; tokens by type, cost, models, outcome, a link to the job's comment or review, the PR; a Total row), reads the issue, splices only its marked block (`<!-- ard:cost:begin -->` to `<!-- ard:cost:end -->`, appended after the human text on first write), writes the body through the effect layer, reads it again to verify, and re-splices and writes once more if another writer replaced the block. A failed table write is logged and retried on a later tick (ten failures, then it waits for the next close-out on the issue); it never fails or delays a close-out. The table holds only counts, dollars, model ids, a fixed outcome vocabulary (`done`, `changes`, `stale`, `needs-human`, `retry`, `usage limit`, `failed: <cause kind | exit code | result>`) and links, never agent text, a reason, a prompt, a log or a host path. It is capped at 5,000 bytes (and by GitHub's 65,536-character body limit): the oldest rows fold into one \"earlier jobs (k)\" row while Total stays exact. A claim that failed before the agent ran has no close-out and no row. The scanner keeps the block across its own body rewrites (it cuts the valid block off, renders, and puts it back), and takes the same per-issue lock. **Residual race:** GitHub has no conditional body write, so there is one GET-to-PATCH round trip in which a human save from a stale editor is overwritten; ard never writes text it did not just read, re-reads to verify, retries once, and GitHub's edit history recovers the lost edit. In dry-run the write goes to the ledger only and the table is not verified or linked. The `costs` loop keeps running while the controller is paused: it is bookkeeping about work already done, so it reports `Claims() == false` like the Reaper. A review's row links to its review because `Effects.CreateReview` returns the review id (stored in the close-out record).\n280-\n281-**The Reaper runs every minute and decides failure, not the agent.** It marks a job failed when the process or pod is gone without a close-out, the deadline passes (it kills the job first), the heartbeat goes quiet for 10 minutes, or the job is `:running` with no record in local state. Every failure goes through close-out, so it is resumable and never double-comments:\n282-\n283-1. **Who acts.** The Reaper kills only a job that a goroutine in this process still drives (its pool slot is held and was not adopted at startup), and only for a deadline or a quiet heartbeat; it records the reason first, and the owner's close-out reads it. A worker keeps its job after the agent exits (tests, fix rounds), so \"exited\" alone never means abandoned. Any job nobody drives (adopted after a restart, or no slot) is closed out by the Reaper itself, and its adopted slot is released. A job whose close-out has started is resumed, never failed.\n284-2. **Close-out.** Commit the partial output to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` (on `main` or the branch, as a normal close-out would), post one comment with the reason, the last 50 redacted log lines and the time the evidence is kept until, then remove `ard:<stage>:<role>:running` and add `ard:executor:failed`. A transient cause instead hands off to the automatic retry, and a usage limit pauses the issue until the reset (see Retries are explicit). A log that cannot be committed (a worker that stopped before its first push has no branch) never turns a retry into a failure.\n285-3. **Retention.** The worktree and job home are kept 24 hours after the job ended, then removed, together with the host logs of a failed job (the raw combined log is already deleted at close-out once its redacted copy is committed). A human extends this by adding `ard:keep` to the issue (or PR, for a review): the Reaper looks again 24 hours later. If it cannot read the issue it keeps everything. Branches are never deleted: closing the draft PR would lose the work a human retry reuses, so a human or a janitor removes them.\n286-4. **`:running` with no record.** Every 5 minutes, for each configured triage, worker and reviewer role, an open issue or PR that has the `:running` label but no running job (and none that ended in the last 10 minutes) gets one comment, `ard:executor:failed` and the label removed. It is a core-API list per role, not a search. It is skipped in dry-run, whose database never matches real labels, and for issues held with `ard:hold`.\n287-\n288-Host log files older than 7 days are pruned hourly. The Reaper keeps running while the controller is paused.\n289-\n290-Successful jobs clean up at close-out: the worktree is removed, the Kubernetes `Job` gets a short TTL, and logs stay 7 days.\n291-\n292-Host job steps run with `GOFLAGS=-modcacherw`, so a Go module cache downloaded into the job home is writable. The module cache is per job and is not shared, because a cache that jobs can write to would let one job poison modules that later jobs build. Cleanup restores owner write permission on the whole job home and worktree before removing them, and never follows symlinks, so read-only trees no longer leak.\n293-\n294-## Worker loop\n295-\n296-A worker implements `plan.md` on its own branch and ends with a draft PR, test results, and a summary on the issue.\n297-\n298-1. **Poll and claim** `ard:worker:<role>` (added by the triage fan-in gate; the worker never adds or removes it) like triage, also skipping `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` and an issue that has a running triage job or any running worker job. Workers rank second for job slots, after reviewers. Before claiming, the loop re-checks `ard:approved` when `require_approval` is on (no label change when it is missing), and the admission: an App-authored issue passes, a human-filed one passes only when its stored body hash still matches, and a changed title or body is re-gated exactly as triage does (one comment, `ard:needs-human`).\n--\n314:## Reviewer loop\n315-\n316-Reviewers run like workers against a PR instead of an issue: they read the change, never edit code, and return a verdict the controller turns into PR comments and a label.\n317-\n318-1. **Poll and claim** open PRs with `ard:review:<role>`, skipping claimed, done, changes, failed and held. Reviewers get first call on free job slots, since each finished review can unblock a merge. Only PRs the App opened are reviewed: the author is the App, the base is `main` and the head is a `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` branch (human PRs are skipped and logged as `not_app_pr`). A PR is also skipped when its issue is closed, held or `ard:needs-human`, when a worker job for the issue is running, when the PR has `ard:tests:failed`, and when the issue's title or body changed after admission (the issue is re-gated exactly as the worker does, not reviewed). Reviewers of different roles run in parallel on one PR.\n319-2. **Pin the head SHA.** Before claiming, the loop fetches the branch into the controller's clone and requires the tip to equal the PR's head SHA; on a mismatch it retries on the next poll. The match also shows the head is this repository's branch, so a fork PR with a colliding branch name is never reviewed. The job's worktree is cut at that commit, so the review describes exactly one commit even if the branch moves.\n320-3. **Run the agent** with `.ard/reviewers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/diff.patch` (the controller's diff from the merge base with `main` to the pinned head, `--stat` then the patch, taken with `--text`, `--no-textconv` and attributes read from the empty tree so a `.gitattributes` entry cannot hide a change, with this issue's `.ard/<issue#>/` left out and cut at 1 MiB), `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, never committed), the committed `.ard/<issue#>/` (plan, triage and worker role files and logs) and the sidecar (security policy, license allowlist). The loop runs every git command against the controller clone, never in the reviewer's worktree (the agent itself may run read-only git there). In round 2 and later it also gets its previous findings and must say which are resolved (review feedback loop).\n321-4. **The agent writes `review-<role>.md`** with `verdict: approve | changes` and `findings:` (`file`, `line`, `severity`, `blocking`, `message`; see the .ard/ contract). It may write nothing else under `.ard/<issue#>/`, and code edits are never collected. An `approve` that carries a blocking finding is treated as `changes` (the job succeeds and the comment says so).\n322-5. **Close-out.** The controller posts one PR review at the pinned commit (event `COMMENT`): findings on lines the PR added are inline comments, every other finding is listed in the review body. If GitHub answers 422 (a line or the commit is not in the PR), it post","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M84E8EKM9Z61D1YTWJWRPAAX/8d7f8cdf-40a1-4f3c-b539-c6edd7579f61/tool-results/b41ujwqiz.txt","persistedOutputSize":34530},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VCCTPJsvkQjzHcJY6HBMYZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
